C5 — tie-break deterministic and order-independent.  MET, by permutation.
  $ cd <scratch>/b2copy && go test -count=1 -v -run TestProbe_TieBreakOrderIndependent ./internal/services/accounts/
    legset 0 owners across 2 permutations: map[zeta:2]
    legset 0 aggregate signatures: map[alpha=0.00;zeta=200.00; total=200.00:2]
    legset 1 owners across 6 permutations: map[c:6]
    legset 1 aggregate signatures: map[a=0.00;b=0.00;c=200.00; total=200.00:6]
    legset 2 owners across 6 permutations: map[n:6]
    legset 2 aggregate signatures: map[m=0.00;n=200.00;x=0.00; total=200.00:6]
    legset 3 owners across 6 permutations: map[dup:6]
    legset 3 aggregate signatures: map[aa=0.00;dup=200.00; total=200.00:6]
    --- PASS
Every permutation of every legset (2-way exact tie, 3-way exact tie, tie at the
newest date behind an older leg, duplicate AccountIDs) yields ONE owner and ONE
aggregate signature; the singleton maps are the evidence. The implementation is
a max over the total order (Date, AccountID), which is order-independent by
construction, and the permutation sweep confirms it empirically.

