OBSERVATIONS (not criterion failures; recorded for the lead's judgement, not
acted on — this checker does not edit).

OBS-1: when the NEWEST leg of a series is an unassigned row (AccountID ""),
the series is now attributed to nobody, where the old code charged the account
holding the older real leg. internal/models/account.go:40 states unassigned
rows are a real state ("A file matching no account leaves its rows' AccountID
empty -- unassigned, never dropped"), so this is reachable.
  $ cd <scratch>/b2copy && go test -run TestDoc_NewestLegUnassigned -v ...
    legs=[usaa@Jun, ""@Jul] -> usaa Minimum=1000.00 (reduction 0.00)
    legs=[""@Jun, usaa@Jul] -> usaa Minimum=800.00  (reduction 200.00)
  $ cd <scratch>/b2old  && (same test)
    legs=[usaa@Jun, ""@Jul] -> usaa Minimum=800.00  (reduction 200.00)
    legs=[""@Jun, usaa@Jul] -> usaa Minimum=800.00  (reduction 200.00)
This is the faithful consequence of the mandated rule (most-recent-leg wins,
applied to the leg set as given), it never over-charges, and it is precisely
what C4's second clause asks for ("must not be attributed to a real account by
accident"). It is under-projection, not double-drain, and C3 as written scopes
to single-account series (verified identical over 82,320 cases). Flagging only
because the brief's C3 phrase "no drift for the common case" could be read to
include a one-account series that also carries unassigned legs.

OBS-2: recurringOwnerAccount's doc comment says it returns "" "when it has no
historical legs at all". It also returns "" when the newest leg carries an
empty AccountID (OBS-1). The comment is narrower than the behaviour. Cosmetic;
no criterion covers it.

OBS-3: an account whose own ID is "" would now match an ownerless series
(`"" != ""` is false, so the item is applied), where the old per-leg filter
skipped it. Demonstrated:
  $ go test -run TestProbe_ZeroIDAccountEdge -v ...
    acct.ID="" empty Transactions    reduction=200.00
    acct.ID="" empty-AccountID legs  reduction=200.00
models.Account.ID is documented as "a stable slug ... must be unique"; both
production call sites (internal/handlers/dashboard/accounts_card.go:190 and
internal/services/mcpsvc/ledger/accounts.go:241) iterate configured accounts,
so an empty-ID Account is not a state the system produces. Noted for
completeness only.

